From b9fe15456ae800bf0f416ad18acf77db784b3dcc Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: arena <arena@localhost>
Date: Mon, 19 Oct 2026 22:08:44 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 1/2] sema, lowering: stop folding library builtins through a
 shadowing binding

A bare call to `strlen`, `memcmp`, `memcpy`, `abs`, ... only denotes the C
library function of that name when nothing in scope shadows it (C11 6.2.1p4).
Three places in LCCC folded or lowered the bare name without asking what the
identifier actually resolved to:

  1. `SemaConstEval::eval_call_const` (`src/frontend/sema/const_eval.rs`) sent
     every bare identifier straight into `common::const_eval`, so in a
     constant-expression or `sizeof` context `strlen("abc")` folded to 3 even
     when `strlen` was a function-pointer object or a GNU nested function in
     scope. With `char vla[strlen("abc") == 103 ? 1 : -1]`, sema folded the
     bound to -1 and rejected a valid VLA with `size of array is negative`.
     Fix: `fold_as_library_function(name)` (and its pure decision seam
     `library_call_is_foldable`) checks `symbols.lookup(name)`:
       - `None` -> fold (ordinary library call);
       - `Some(CType::Function(_))` -> fold only at file scope
         (`lookup_depth(name).unwrap_or(0) == 0`), declining block-scope
         function declarations so GNU nested functions are never folded away;
       - `Some(_)` -> decline (an object, typedef, or enumerator shadows the
         library name).
  2. `Lowerer::is_func_ptr_variable` (`src/ir/lowering/expr_calls.rs`) exempted
     any file-scope object in `globals` whose name also appeared in
     `known_functions`. Because `declare_implicit_functions` pre-populates
     `known_functions` with the standard library names (`strlen`, `memcpy`,
     `malloc`, `printf`, ...) and with nested-function basenames, a file-scope
     pointer object such as `static size_t (*strlen)(const char *) = my_strlen;`
     always hit the veto: at -O0 `lower_function_call` emitted a direct
     `call strlen` against the data object and the program jumped into its own
     data (SIGSEGV, exit 139). Dropping the `!self.known_functions.contains(name)`
     veto in both global branches fixes the call target; C11 6.7p4 already
     forbids declaring a function and an object of the same name at file scope,
     so no valid direct call is lost.
  3. `Lowerer::eval_const_expr` (`src/ir/lowering/const_eval.rs`) and
     `Lowerer::try_fold_builtin_or_attr_call` (`src/ir/lowering/expr_calls.rs`)
     folded unannotated calls (`strlen` in a branch condition, `abs` through a
     file-scope pointer object) before reaching `lower_function_call`. Guard
     `eval_const_expr` with `self.library_name_is_shadowed(name)`
     (`is_func_ptr_variable(name) || lookup_nested_function(name).is_some()`)
     and `try_fold_builtin_or_attr_call` with
     `callee_is_local || self.is_func_ptr_variable(name)`.

Because every guard keys on the identifier as spelled at the call site, an
explicit `__builtin_*` call continues to fold even when the bare basename is
shadowed in the same scope, matching GCC 14.2 and Clang 23.1.3.

Tests:
  - tests/regression/builtin_call_shadowed_by_function_pointer.c (-O2)
  - tests/regression/builtin_call_shadowed_by_function_pointer_o0.c (-O0)
  - tests/regression/builtin_call_shadowed_by_nested_function.c (-O2)
  - six unit tests in `sema::const_eval::library_name_shadowing_tests`
