From 6a53d07e431ab35d3f71cb9173167caff9a2f910 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: arena <arena@localhost>
Date: Mon, 19 Oct 2026 22:08:45 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 2/2] passes, const_eval: answer strcmp/memcmp of literals with
 the sign both vendors give

`memcmp("z", "a", 1)` printed 25 out of LCCC and 1 out of gcc 14.2 and clang
23.1.3 -- measured at -O0 and -O2 alike, and likewise `strcmp("c", "a")` (2
versus 1) and `memcmp("abc", "abx", 3)` (-21 versus -1). C11 7.1.4p1 fixes only
the SIGN of a comparison result, so both answers are permitted, but a program
that prints one has one observable value per compiler, and LCCC's was the odd
one out against both vendors: it left the call in and reported glibc's byte
difference.

Two layers, because the two answer in different contexts:

  * `common::const_eval`'s fold table gains `strcmp`/`memcmp` (and the reserved
    spellings) next to the `strlen` arm that was already there, with
    `narrow_compare_sign` as the shared comparator: unsigned-char comparison,
    first difference decides, `memcmp` takes embedded NULs as data and stops
    after its length argument, `strcmp` stops at the terminator. A length that
    is not a compile-time value, is negative, or reaches past what a literal
    spells declines into the call -- declining is never wrong.
    This covers the constant-expression contexts sema evaluates: static
    initializers, array sizes, case labels, `_Static_assert`, enumerators.
  * `constant_fold::fold_strlen_literals` becomes `fold_string_library_calls`
    and folds the same family over the IR, which is where a call in a runtime
    expression can still be answered (sema never annotates those). It runs
    after inlining, so a literal that reaches a comparison through a parameter
    folds too. `CallInfo::assume_library` is the whole admissibility test and
    `passes::builtin_policy::apply` sets it right after lowering, so
    `-fno-builtin[-<name>]` keeps the call, an explicit `__builtin_*` still
    folds, and the A13/A14 contract is untouched. The -O1 tier runs it as well
    (`assume_library` is already decided there and the
    `__builtin_constant_p` resolution below wants the folded values); -O0
    deliberately keeps the call -- that tier skips every value-changing pass so
    a debugger can step into the library.

`strncmp` is NOT folded, and that is a measurement, not an oversight: gcc 14.2
leaves glibc's difference (`strncmp("cd", "ab", 1)` is 2) while clang 23.1.3
normalizes to 1. With the vendors split there is no agreed answer to move to, so
the call stays and LCCC remains on gcc's side -- the differential corpus's
oracle. A unit test pins the refusal so the asymmetry cannot be "fixed" by
accident.

Measured after the change, `memcmp`/`strcmp` of literal arguments:
gcc 14.2, clang 23.1.3 and LCCC all answer 1 / -1 / 0 at -O1 and -O2; at -O0
LCCC keeps the call and answers with glibc's difference. The 592-cell shadowing
matrix loses its last two-vendor-divergent cluster: 18 divergent cells against
gcc 14.2 become 12 (all of them programs that redefine or redirect a reserved
library name, which C11 7.1.3 makes undefined), and against clang 23.1.3 the
divergent count is 0.

Tests: tests/regression/library_compare_sign.c (differential against gcc,
including the strncmp rows and a runtime row that must keep printing glibc's
difference) and six unit tests carrying the measured vendor values.
